title: A hold is tied to the Claude process that made it, so it ends when that process does, with or without 'release'.
pid: bb1710df-83f1-4854-9928-f096b2701432
body: |2-
  'hold' walks up from its shell to the first process named 'claude'
  (Claude Code's grep runs as 'claude.exe', which does not match) and
  names the file in 'data/claude/pacer/holds/' by its pid, with its start
  time, so a reused pid is not mistaken for it. Whoever next reads the
  holds deletes a lapsed one and logs that in 'pacer.log'. The subagents
  of a session run in its process, so they share its hold; a session
  cut off by the pacer's timeout or a container stop lets go as it dies.
  A session started by the pacer is named by its stamp, through
  'PACER_SESSION', which the pacer gives it; any other is "a session the
  pacer did not start".
